module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Kaehler.Basic
public import Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.CommRing
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Algebra.ZMod
import Sheaf.RingTheory.JacobiZariskiHomologySequence

/-!
# Differentials of a presentation adjoining p-th roots

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, proof of Proposition Tag 07E5, (2) => (4)
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-pth-root-presentation-differentials).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open scoped TensorProduct

namespace KaehlerDifferential

variable (p n : ℕ) (A : Type u) [CommRing A] [Algebra (ZMod p) A] (a : Fin n → A)

local notation "P" => MvPolynomial (Fin n) A
local notation "I" => Ideal.span (Set.range (fun j : Fin n =>
  (MvPolynomial.X j : P) ^ p - MvPolynomial.C (a j)))
local notation "B" => P ⧸ I

/-- Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, proof of Proposition Tag 07E5, (2) => (4).
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-pth-root-presentation-differentials)
The relative differentials have the indicated basis, and the absolute differential sequence
has kernel generated by the differentials of the coefficients.

Proof, following the node's six steps:
1. The polynomial differential basis (lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-ring-kahler-basis) gives
   d(X_j^p - a_j) = 0 relatively over A. By Leibniz, every element of I has zero
   differential after tensoring with B (def-weil-pbc-kahler-differentials).
2. The conormal exact sequence (lem-weil-pbc-jacobi-zariski-differential-maps) therefore
   identifies the base-changed polynomial differentials with the relative differentials.
   Transport the polynomial basis using lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-ring-kahler-basis-any-variables.
3. The first homology of this presentation is I/I^2, generated by the classes of X_j^p-a_j,
   using lem-weil-pbc-nl-presentation-h1-functoriality.
4. Compare with the canonical presentation A[B] -> B: send X_j to the symbol of its image,
   and lift every element of B to a polynomial for the reverse map. Independence and
   composition in lem-weil-pbc-nl-presentation-h1-functoriality make the induced H1 maps
   inverse. Thus these relation classes generate the canonical H1 of
   def-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map.
5. The Jacobi-Zariski sequence for ZMod p -> A -> B
   (lem-weil-pbc-jacobi-zariski-homology-sequence) sends each relation class to
   -1 tensor d(a_j), by its coefficient formula. This agrees with the source up to sign.
6. The image of the connecting map is consequently the span of 1 tensor d(a_j), the image
   of the linear combination map below. Exactness at the next term and surjectivity of the
   last map follow from the same Jacobi-Zariski sequence. -/
theorem pthRootPresentation_differentials [Fact p.Prime] :
    (∃ b : Module.Basis (Fin n) B Ω[B⁄A],
      ∀ j : Fin n, b j = D A B (Ideal.Quotient.mk I (MvPolynomial.X j))) ∧
    Function.Exact
      (Finsupp.linearCombination B
        (fun j : Fin n => (1 : B) ⊗ₜ[A] D (ZMod p) A (a j)))
      (mapBaseChange (ZMod p) A B) ∧
    Function.Exact (mapBaseChange (ZMod p) A B) (map (ZMod p) A B B) ∧
    Function.Surjective (map (ZMod p) A B B) := by
  sorry

end KaehlerDifferential
